<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>SBR.Sim</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!--
      SERVER GC, SHIPPED AS THE DEFAULT (Allen's ruling, 2026-08-15).

      Measured on the 22-core box, one 2,000-run batch, Release: 3.3 s at 16 workers under server GC
      against 5.4 s under the default workstation GC, for +24 MB peak. The sim is an embarrassingly
      parallel allocator-bound workload — every run builds its own object graph and drops it — which
      is precisely the shape workstation GC serves worst: one collection heap shared by every worker
      makes allocation a contention point, and the campaign's wall time is mostly threads queueing
      for it. Server GC gives each core its own heap and collects them in parallel.

      WHY THE PROJECT FILE AND NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE. This property is compiled into
      SBR.Sim.runtimeconfig.json beside the binary, so the setting travels with the build to every
      machine and every shell. `DOTNET_gcServer=1` would deliver the same speed only for whoever
      remembered to export it; an 85-minute campaign that is 60% slower because a variable was
      missing from one terminal is a configuration bug that looks like a hardware problem. A
      campaign's cost should be a property of the artifact, not of the operator.

      The env var still WINS over this setting when it is set explicitly, which is deliberate and is
      what the GC-identity proof uses: DOTNET_gcServer=0 runs the workstation arm off this same
      build. GC mode is recorded in the report header (see WorkerPolicy.Describe) so an artifact
      always names the mode it was produced under.

      NOTHING BELOW THE HEADER MOVES. Proven, not assumed: report bodies at 1 and 16 workers, under
      workstation and server GC, are byte-identical by SHA-256 — GC mode is a scheduling and memory
      concern, and the harness reduces every aggregate sequentially from a pre-sized array.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\engine\SBR.Engine.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
